From 3f16507eff3224533823899f2cdca09082a55df0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MaiRat <enrico.bartky@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 06:41:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Resolve property escapes, evaluate v-mode class sets, fold
 from the UCD, guard recursion
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Track 2 of the Broiler.JS gaps roadmap asks for three things Broiler.Regex parsed but
did not implement, and they turn out to be one problem: every character class is a set,
and the engine had no set.

`\p{…}` / `\P{…}` now resolve against the pinned Broiler.Unicode range tables — the same
generated UCD revision the JavaScript layer's translator reads, so the two cannot
disagree about a property's members. General_Category (lone value and the `gc=`
dimension), binary properties with their ECMAScript aliases, `Script`,
`Script_Extensions`, and the UTS #51 properties of strings all resolve; an unknown name
is a located syntax error rather than a silent mis-match.

`v`-mode class-set expressions are evaluated rather than skipped: nesting, `&&`, `--`
and `\q{…}` string alternatives, over a new CodePointSet that carries the union,
intersection, difference and complement the grammar needs. The ordering that matters is
§22.2.1 MaybeSimpleCaseFolding *before* §22.2.2.9 CharacterComplement, whose universe under
`vi` is only the code points that fold to themselves — the reason `[^\p{Lu}]` matches
neither `A` nor `a` under `vi` while it matches `a` under `ui`.

Canonicalize is table-driven from the Unicode Character Database instead of `char`/`Rune`
casing plus five hand-written special cases. `İ` and `ı` have no simple case folding, so
neither is case-equivalent to an ASCII `i` any more; and WordCharacters widens `\w` under
`iu` from the fold table, so `/\W/iu` rejects `ſ` and `\b` agrees with `\w` about it.

Six Annex B boundaries came out of differential testing rather than the roadmap: `\u{…}`
without the `u` flag, a bare `]`/`{`/`}` in Unicode mode, a class escape as a range
endpoint, `\-` inside a class, a quantifier after an assertion, and `\k<n>` in a pattern
that declares no group name.

A stack-depth guard makes the matcher crash-safe. The continuation-passing matcher
recurses once per quantifier iteration, so a repeat over a long subject would overflow the
runtime stack. Repeat now checks the remaining stack (RuntimeHelpers.TryEnsureSufficient-
ExecutionStack) and throws RegexOverflowException — which unwinds cleanly — instead of
crashing the process; a host may catch it and fall back to an iterative engine. This
matters once the JavaScript layer routes every Unicode-mode pattern here.

250 tests, up from 57. Beyond those, 220,172 differential pattern/flag/input cases against
V8 on match index and every capture. Two mismatches remain, both deliberate `vi`
divergences pinned by a test, both recorded in the README.

The roadmap's "Replace the translator" section records the Broiler.JS-side integration
work — one match-data abstraction, a routed pattern that needs no .NET translation, and
routing widened to all of Unicode mode — and narrows what is left before the translator
can be dropped.
